@@ -246,5 +246,8 @@ The Colossus-style bootstrap recursion bottoms out here: etcd depends on nothing
 | Telemetry | OpenTelemetry (OTLP) | `telemetry` (ADR-0012) |
 | Simulation testing | madsim / turmoil | `testkit` (ADR-0009) |
 | Composition / the binary | wires all concretes | `server` |
+| Blackbox validation | separate `wyrd-validate` binary, no `wyrd-*` dependency | `validate` (proposal 0017) |
 
 The dependency rule (ADR-0010): implementations and consumers depend on `traits`, never on each other's concretes. Only `server` knows the concrete backends, which is what makes "swap redb for FoundationDB" or "in-memory for etcd" a composition change rather than a refactor. The seam earned its keep in M4: swapping the production backend from TiKV to FoundationDB (ADR-0042) touched the `server` wiring and added a crate, and changed no consumer.
+
+`validate` sits outside that rule on purpose: it is a client of a running deployment, not a part of one, and depends on no `wyrd-*` crate (proposal 0017 §9). Its command-line surface is ten required flags — `--endpoint`, `--region`, `--bucket`, `--scenario`, `--duration`, `--workers`, `--seed`, `--out`, `--run-id`, `--driver-placement` — with S3 credentials taken from `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`/`AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`, else `WYRD_S3_ACCESS_KEY`/`WYRD_S3_SECRET_KEY`, else refused. Parsing is strict, unlike `wyrd`'s: an unknown flag, a repeated flag, a stray positional, or a `--`-prefixed token in a value slot is refused by name rather than absorbed. Today the binary echoes its resolved configuration (the access-key id, never the secret) and exits without issuing a request; the S3 client arrives with #741.
